<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilkhanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ilkhanid, Ilkhanids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ilkhanate was a Mongol state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a> in the mid-thirteenth century, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and neighbouring lands. Its rulers later converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and under Ghazan carried out major reforms. It has its own profile on the site, was a rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and was one of the successor khanates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilkhanate.jpg" alt="Ilkhanate" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historical_Atlas_of_Iran_-_Plate_No._17_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ilkhanate arose in the south-western territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and was known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> as the Hülegü Ulus, the people or state of Hülegü. Its realm was officially called the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Irān-zamin. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> declared war on the Anushtegin dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> in 1219, the Mongols overran Iran, and by 1231 an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chormaqan">Chormaqan</a>, sent by the Great Khan Ögedei, had crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni">Jalal al-Din Mangburni</a>.</p><p>Hülegü, a son of Tolui, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> and Kublai, was given a fifth of the Mongol army to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. He destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismaili state</a> in 1256 and the Abbasid Caliphate in 1258, and inherited the West Asian part of the empire after Möngke died in 1259.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hülegü advanced as far as Gaza and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1260, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>'s death forced him to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> for the kuriltai. The small force he left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> beat back an invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and one by Ghiyas-ud-din Baraq of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. The Mamluks defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a> in 1277 and again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in 1281.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a> the realm's finances were ruined, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>'s attempt to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> on the Yuan model largely failed. After Gaykhatu was overthrown in 1295 and his cousin Baydu removed within a year, Ghazan took the throne and was the first ilkhan to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-bahadur-khan">Abu Sa'id Bahadur Khan</a>, enthroned in 1316, the realm faced rebellions and invasions. In the 1330s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> ravaged it, and Abu Sa'id, the last ilkhan, died of the plague in 1335. Short-lived khans followed, and in 1357 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, ending what remained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which excluded the native population from high office, the Ilkhanids governed through a Central Asian and Persian administration working with Turco-Mongol military officers. Not every administrator was Muslim or from the old families: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> from 1288 to 1291, Sa'ad al-Dawla, was a Jew, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a> was a Jewish convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Persian bureaucrats promoted the idea of kingship over the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and historians presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> as heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><p>The period produced many works of history, the best known being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>' al-tawarikh, commissioned by Ghazan and presented to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a> in 1307. Hülegü's astronomers built an observatory at Maragheh. Western European courts repeatedly sought an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> but never concluded a formal one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilkhanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mongol alliance that had protected Cilicia failed it in the fourteenth century as the Crusader states and the <b>Ilkhanate</b> disintegrated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate benefited through tolls and taxes on goods passing through its territory, although its frequent wars with the Yuan and the <b>Ilkhanate</b> repeatedly disrupted the routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongols made Tabriz the capital of the <b>Ilkhanate</b> in the second half of the thirteenth century, Trebizond became a principal Black Sea outlet for the route from Persia, through which silk, spices and other goods reached Western merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1262 the khans fought the <b>Ilkhanate of Persia</b> over Azerbaijan and the Caucasus, a war that drew Berke into alliance with the Mamluk sultan Baybars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Eastern Georgia remained under the <b>Ilkhanate</b>, which executed Demetrius II in 1289.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In diplomacy the Mamluk state dealt with the Golden Horde, the <b>Ilkhanate</b>, the Ottomans and European powers such as Venice and Aragon, and it counted for much in regional politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
